1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,880
Try locking onto an enemy in Zelda.

2
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:04,480
Link keeps facing it while moving sideways.

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:07,640
What did a Kyoto ninja show contribute?

4
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:10,040
Keeping Link visible in third person

5
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,880
meant solving camera and combat problems together.

6
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,100
Even in Super Mario 64,

7
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:16,140
trying to line up with a sign

8
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:18,120
could leave you circling it.

9
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:20,920
A developer looking for swordplay ideas

10
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:22,160
went to a show.

11
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,220
A ninja kept a taut chain between opponents

12
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:26,120
while circling the other fighter.

13
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,640
The developer took note of that movement.

14
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,620
Making opponents easier to target

15
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:33,240
had been discussed since Super Mario 64.

16
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:35,720
Another developer found a solution at the show:

17
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:37,360
taking turns to attack.

18
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:39,960
While one fought, the others waited.

19
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:41,680
One example of this design is

20
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:44,640
the Forest Temple fight against two Stalfos.

21
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,240
Focus on one opponent and fight it,

22
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:49,480
then select the next.

23
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:50,680
Think of the idea from the chain

24
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:53,200
as an invisible connection.

25
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,480
The direction toward the opponent is the reference.

26
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:57,440
Moving forward brings you closer.

27
00:00:57,440 --> 00:00:59,480
Moving sideways circles the opponent.

28
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,840
Find an opening, then jump in and attack.

29
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,640
Movement around a target,

30
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,880
and managing several attackers:

31
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:07,560
two insights from the same show

32
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:10,280
helped make three-dimensional combat easier to read.
